worklog/worklog_yixun/exp_22_loc_meshgrid_claude/loc_meshgrid_worklog.md:619:- **Ambiguities resolved (each a reading, disclosed)** — (a) "Include the pinned anchor digest/query digest in evidence": published G0's whole anchor block and key-set result AND made it a G3 / G3D clause (no or unpinned anchor → FAIL / incomplete), since a published field that nothing judges would not stop a wrong anchor. (b) "serialize the complete replay-partition record": `chunk["replay_partition"]` became the record dict (contract change; its `partition` is the old list); each chunk now also carries its per-key facts and the executed partition its raw counts, because the per-key aggregate alone cannot show a key present on one side in a single chunk. (c) "recomputes every PASS clause … FROM THE SERIALIZED JSON ONLY": the verifier also checks cross-field equalities that hold by construction in the driver (comparison inputs vs the binding, declared == the binding's `fa_partition`, every summary vs its parts), so it is at least as strict as the gate on real evidence; unit-test reports finalized at fixture numbers are not real evidence and do not re-verify. (d) "G1, G2, G0 and GV if the serialized fields already allow it cheaply — say which": G1, G2, GV yes as they stood; G0 no — it needed its frozen digests per query and the published binding (two additive fields) — added rather than leave G0 out. (e) "the run summary's ledger block … equal to the ledger": exactly equal at a merge; at a reopen the summary may be absent or describe exactly a PREFIX of the segments — the only legitimate stale state (an invocation finished, a later one crashed after adding a segment); exact equality at reopen would make that crash unrecoverable. (f) "every count a non-negative integer": literal (zero admitted, bools refused), plus the canonical-serialization round trip. (g) NIT 5 "document … or stage/quarantine rows": the brief's explicit marker, not staging (staging would change the row-publication path of a 3-day pass); the marker is also written when the BEFORE-ROW check fails (a disclosed superset: same predicate family, same incomplete rows). (h) A6 (iv) "refused by merge and report": the report's refusal needed one line in `meshgrid_report.py` (no engine function reads a run directory for the report); `meshgrid_crossarm` admission inherits it. (i) HIGH 3's "`fa_partition_census` (merge and report)": read as the merge and its receipt (`merge_report.json`); the per-arm report never read the fa ledger and still does not. (j) NIT 6's `exit 1`: right for a launch script under `set -euo pipefail`; in an interactive shell it would close the terminal, so the list says to wrap the checks in `( … )` there. (k) "a provenance clause flipped": tested as a flipped provenance FACT (`tracked_clean: false`) and as a flipped `verdict_clauses.provenance` flag.
worklog/worklog_yixun/exp_22_loc_meshgrid_claude/loc_meshgrid_worklog.md:662:- **Change** — (a) `fa_partition_census` renumbers the merged segments globally `0..n−1` (shard order as given, then each shard's own order), each keeping `shard` (the shard dir as given) and `shard_segment_index` (its shard-local index), and returns `{ledger, receipt}` only after verifying both exactly as the reader verifies a merged run — so the merge writes nothing it would itself refuse; `merge_shards` writes them unchanged (receipt keys as before: executed, violations, counts_sha256, per_shard). (b) `read_fa_ledger`: a directory that publishes `merge_report.json` is a MERGED run, read only exactly (a reopen is refused: a merged directory is never resumed into); `_merged_reasons`: every segment names its shard and a real-int shard-local index, each shard is ONE contiguous block numbered `0..k−1` in order, and the receipt is exactly what the segments yield (the summed executed block, violations, counts digest, and each shard's census `{dir, counts_sha256, n_segments}` in shard order); a missing ledger, a missing / null receipt or any difference is a named refusal. A vanilla run (`declared` None) may carry no ledger, no run-summary block and no NON-NULL receipt; absent (the Aug-2026 P1 merged run has no such key — checked on the real directory, read-only) and null (what every YAW merge writes) are admitted. (c) `meshgrid_report.evaluate_run` calls `me.read_fa_ledger(run_dir, binding["fa_partition"], exact=True)` right after the binding is recomputed — a merged BF run is joined to its receipt, a single-shard BF run to its run summary, a vanilla run must carry neither; `meshgrid_crossarm.admit_run` inherits the refusal through `evaluate_run`. The report change is one call and two comment lines; every P1 / vanilla report output is unchanged (the P1 golden test passes untouched).
worklog/worklog_yixun/exp_22_loc_meshgrid_claude/loc_meshgrid_worklog.md:670:- **Binding chosen, and why it is not literally Codex's in-row stamp (disclosed)** — the first GREEN attempt put the stamp IN the row (`row["fa_ledger_state"]`): 9 crossarm tests then failed, because `meshgrid_crossarm.pair_runs` refuses every row field outside its `PAIRING_WHITELIST` (BF rows would differ from P1 / YAW rows on `row.fa_ledger_state`), and that whitelist lives in the PROTECTED `meshgrid_crossarm.py`. Hiding the field from the crossarm in the report would have circumvented the protected gate. So the stamp lives BESIDE the row — `<out-dir>/fa_ledger_stamps/<room>/q<position>.json = {segment_index, n_queries, counts_sha256, query_id}` — written atomically after the ledger write and before the row write, copied by the merge with its row; a BF row keeps exactly the vanilla row schema (tested), vanilla passes write no stamp. **Equivalence against Codex's probe**: a row's own `row_sha256` is a self-digest anyone can recompute, so Codex's in-row stamp is defeated only by rewriting the ledger AND re-signing the last row; the sidecar is defeated only by rewriting the ledger AND the last row's stamp file — the same two-file coordinated edit. Both refuse the probe (truncated tail, recomputed self-digests, summary deleted) at the reopen, before a resume writes anything. The one-line alternative (whitelisting `row.fa_ledger_state` in `meshgrid_crossarm.PAIRING_WHITELIST`, beside `launch_record_sha256`) is the Planner's to choose; it was NOT made.
